@prefix cd: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/ontology/> .
@prefix ex: <https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/resource/> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/specifications/level4-moments-lesson> {
  ex:l4-raw1 a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "First raw moment: average level relative to zero"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Concept**\n\nA moment averages a power. The first empirical raw moment averages x itself: **a₁ = mean(x)**. Raw means measured from the chosen zero, not from the sample mean.\n\n**Calculation**\n\nFor 98, 99, 100, 101, 102% recovery: sum = 500%, n = 5, so **a₁ = 100%**. Units are the original measurement units.\n\nThe population counterpart is E[X], when it exists. Our finite-sample descriptors use **1/n**, not an automatic bias correction."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw1-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "First empirical raw moment"@en ; cd:latex "a_1=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i=\\bar{x}" ; cd:hasSource ex:l4-source-nist-skew-kurt ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw1-use a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "First moment, strength: track the control level"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: repeated control readings**\n\n100 synthetic normal-like responses. **a₁ = 50 a.u.** summarizes their average level.\n\n**Strength**\n\nUseful for tracking a level or mean bias against a reference. Adding a calibration offset of **+5 a.u.** to every reading changes a₁ to **55 a.u.**, exactly as expected.\n\n**Limit**\n\nThis says nothing about repeatability by itself. One extreme value also shifts the mean: it is not a robust centre."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw1-limit a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "First moment, limit: the mean can lie in a gap"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: two response groups**\n\n100 synthetic detector readings from two narrow groups near 40 and 60 a.u.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nTheir first raw moment is still **50 a.u.**, just as in the normal-like series. But almost no readings are near that value.\n\n==Average level is not necessarily a typical observation.== The first moment does not encode modes, spread or the mechanism behind the two groups."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw2 a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Second raw moment: average squared magnitude"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Concept and calculation**\n\nSquare each reading, then average. Positive and negative readings both contribute positively. The result has **squared units**.\n\nFor 98, 99, 100, 101, 102% recovery, squared values sum to **50,010**: **a₂ = 10,002 %²**.\n\nUseful when squared signal magnitude matters, for example a mean-square signal relative to a defined zero. Its square root is the root-mean-square value. ==a₂ is not the variance.=="@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw2-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Second empirical raw moment and decomposition"@en ; cd:latex "a_2=\\frac1n\\sum_i x_i^2=\\bar{x}^{\\,2}+m_2" ; cd:hasSource ex:l4-source-nist-skew-kurt ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw2-use a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Second moment, strength: a mean-square summary"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: 100 control responses**\n\nMean **50 a.u.**, sample SD **10 a.u.** The divisor-n central second moment is **99 a.u.²**.\n\n**Calculation**\n\na₂ = 50² + 99 = **2,599 a.u.²**; RMS ≈ **50.980 a.u.**\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nThe mean-square signal combines average level and variation. It is useful only if both are relevant to the question and zero has a meaningful definition."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw2-limit a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Second moment, limit: offsets dominate"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: change only the calibration origin**\n\nAdd 100 a.u. to the same 100 readings. Mean becomes **150**, while m₂ remains **99 a.u.²**.\n\n**Calculation**\n\na₂ becomes 150² + 99 = **22,599 a.u.²**, despite unchanged spread. The diagram shows the original readings.\n\n==Do not compare raw second moments as pure repeatability measures.== Also, the uniform and bimodal series share a₁ = 50 and a₂ = 2,599: two moments still do not identify shape."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw3 a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Third raw moment: signed cubes about zero"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Concept and calculation**\n\nCube each value and average. Cubing retains the sign and magnifies large magnitudes. Units are **measurement units cubed**.\n\nFor 98, 99, 100, 101, 102% recovery, **a₃ = 1,000,600 %³**. This symmetric sample has a large positive raw third moment simply because its centre is far above zero.\n\n==A raw third moment is not skewness.== It mixes level, spread and signed deviations."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw3-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Third empirical raw moment"@en ; cd:latex "a_3=\\frac1n\\sum_i x_i^3=\\bar{x}^{\\,3}+3\\bar{x}m_2+m_3" ; cd:hasSource ex:l4-source-nist-skew-kurt ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw3-use a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Third moment, strength: probe signed residual contributions"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: use a scientifically meaningful zero**\n\nFor 100 synthetic normal-like responses, define residuals as response **minus the reference 50 a.u.** The positive and negative cubes cancel: residual a₃ ≈ **0**.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nSigned cubes reveal imbalance in large deviations when the origin is justified. For the right-skewed series with the same mean and SD, residual a₃ ≈ **1,733.015 a.u.³**.\n\nThe diagram shows that right-skewed series on its original response scale. The sign summarizes imbalance, not its analytical cause."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw3-limit a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Third moment, limit: positive cubes do not prove skew"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: the symmetric 100-reading control**\n\nOn its original response scale, **a₃ = 139,850 a.u.³**, even though the signed central third moment is zero.\n\n**Why?**\n\n50³ + 3 × 50 × 99 + 0 = **139,850**. Translation changes raw moments. Large observations dominate cubes, so even central third moments can be unstable in small samples.\n\n==Separate the origin from the shape question.== Zero third moment alone does not establish symmetry."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw4 a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Fourth raw moment: amplify large magnitudes"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Concept and calculation**\n\nRaise every reading to the fourth power and average. Contributions are nonnegative and large magnitudes receive very high weight. Units are **measurement units to the fourth power**.\n\nFor 98, 99, 100, 101, 102% recovery, **a₄ = 100,120,006.8 %⁴**.\n\nUseful in mathematical models involving fourth powers or for probing extreme residual contributions relative to a justified zero. ==It is not kurtosis and is not a pure tail descriptor on an arbitrary response scale.=="@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw4-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Fourth empirical raw moment"@en ; cd:latex "a_4=\\frac1n\\sum_i x_i^4=\\bar{x}^{\\,4}+6\\bar{x}^{\\,2}m_2+4\\bar{x}m_3+m_4" ; cd:hasSource ex:l4-source-nist-skew-kurt ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw4-use a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Fourth moment, strength: distinguish extreme residual weight"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: compare residuals about 50 a.u.**\n\nAll four constructed detector series have n = 100 and m₂ = **99 a.u.²**. Their residual fourth moments differ:\n\n- Normal-like: **27,777.61 a.u.⁴**.\n- Right-skewed: **65,297.53 a.u.⁴**.\n- Uniform: **17,639.45 a.u.⁴**.\n- Two clusters: **10,918.86 a.u.⁴**.\n\n**Interpretation:** fourth powers discriminate extreme-deviation weight that the first two moments miss. They do not reconstruct the full distribution."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-raw4-limit a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Fourth moment, limit: one deviation can dominate"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: a reference-centred residual**\n\nChanging a residual from **2 to 10 a.u.** multiplies its fourth-power contribution from **16 to 10,000 a.u.⁴**: a factor of **625**.\n\n**Limit**\n\nThis sensitivity can flag scientifically important extremes, but also magnifies contamination and sampling variability. Check the actual observations and their provenance.\n\nThe right-skewed 100-reading example is shown. A finite sample always has finite moments; this does not prove that the corresponding population moments exist."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-central a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Central moments: move the origin to the sample mean"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Concept**\n\nReplace xᵢ with its deviation **xᵢ − mean(x)** before taking powers. This removes an additive offset.\n\n- **m₁ = 0**: deviations balance algebraically.\n- **m₂**: average squared deviation, divisor **n**.\n- **m₃**: signed cubic deviation balance.\n- **m₄**: fourth-power deviation weight.\n\nFor n > 1, sample variance s² = n m₂/(n−1). ==Do not apply the n−1 correction mechanically to every moment.== Population central moments are expectations and require existence."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-central-worked a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Central moments: calculate orders 1–4"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Five recovery measurements: 98, 99, 100, 101, 102%**\n\nMean = **100%**; deviations = **−2, −1, 0, 1, 2 percentage points**.\n\n- First: (−2−1+0+1+2)/5 = **0**.\n- Second: (4+1+0+1+4)/5 = **2**.\n- Third: (−8−1+0+1+8)/5 = **0**.\n- Fourth: (16+1+0+1+16)/5 = **6.8**.\n\nThe sample variance is **10/4 = 2.5**, not m₂ = 2. Adding a constant changes raw moments, but leaves all these central moments unchanged. Multiplying values by c multiplies mᵣ by cʳ."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-standardized a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Standardized moments: remove the unit and scale"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Concept and calculation**\n\nFor m₂ > 0, standardize deviations by √m₂ and average their powers. Orders 1 and 2 become **0 and 1**.\n\nOrder 3 is descriptive sample skewness **g₁ = m₃/m₂^(3/2)**. Order 4 is descriptive kurtosis **K = m₄/m₂²**; excess kurtosis is **K−3**. The normal population benchmark is K = 3, not a promise for every normal sample.\n\nFor the five recoveries: **g₁ = 0**, **K = 6.8/4 = 1.7**, excess **−1.3**.\n\n==Undefined if m₂ = 0.== These divisor-n descriptive measures are not the bias-adjusted estimators returned by every software package."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-standardized-formula a cd:MathExpression ; cd:formulaLabel "Empirical standardized central moment"@en ; cd:latex "b_r=\\frac{m_r}{m_2^{r/2}}=\\frac1n\\sum_i\\left(\\frac{x_i-\\bar{x}}{\\sqrt{m_2}}\\right)^r,\\qquad m_2>0" ; cd:hasSource ex:l4-source-nist-skew-kurt ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-skew-use a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Skewness: a signed shape summary, not a diagnosis"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: 100 right-skewed detector responses**\n\nWith m₂ = **99**, m₃ ≈ **1,733.015**, descriptive g₁ ≈ **1.759**.\n\n**Strength:** positive g₁ summarizes the stronger upper-deviation contribution, independent of the measurement unit and additive origin.\n\n**Limit:** it neither proves contamination nor gives an exact tail probability. A few observations can dominate. The uniform, normal-like and bimodal examples all have g₁ ≈ 0 despite different shapes."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-skew-zero a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Zero skewness does not imply symmetry"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Exact 50-residual counterexample**\n\n5 residuals equal −3, 25 equal −1, and 20 equal +2 a.u. Mean = (−15−25+40)/50 = **0**.\n\n**Calculate**\n\nm₃ = [5(−27)+25(−1)+20(8)]/50 = **0**; m₂ = **3**; therefore g₁ = **0**.\n\n**But the sample is asymmetric:** −3 has no matching +3 group; +2 has no matching −2 group. Signed cubes cancel without mirror symmetry. ==Inspect the distribution, not just the coefficient.=="@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-kurt-use a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Kurtosis: extreme standardized deviations, not peak height"@en ; cd:bodyFormat "markdown" ; cd:body "**Scenario: same mean and SD, four response shapes**\n\nDescriptive K / excess K−3:\n\n- Normal-like: **2.834 / −0.166**.\n- Right-skewed: **6.662 / 3.662**.\n- Uniform: **1.800 / −1.200**.\n- Bimodal: **1.114 / −1.886**.\n\n**Strength:** dimensionless fourth-power weighting distinguishes extremes relative to spread.\n\n**Limit:** not a direct measurement of peak height, number of modes, tail probability or normality. The two-cluster sample has low K but two narrow peaks. Values are descriptive and sensitive to extreme observations."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-moment-lab a cd:Exercise ; skos:prefLabel "R lab: mean, variance, kurtosis and skewness"@en ; cd:body "Three synthetic shapes, **100 values each**. Rows compare shapes; columns show **points, boxplot, histogram**.\n\n- [!] **Run:** switch **metric**: mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis.\n- [!] **Change:** vary **offset**, **scale_factor**, then **extreme**, one at a time.\n- [?] **Explain:** what changes? Why does **m2** differ from **var(x)**?\n\n==Geometric guides do not guarantee coverage.==\n\nBase R only. Local execution; no package download."@en ; cd:hasCodeExample ex:l4-moment-code ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l4-moment-code a cd:CodeExample ; cd:programmingLanguage "r" ; cd:editable true ; cd:executable true ; cd:showsResource ex:arithmetic-mean, ex:empirical-central-moment, ex:sample-skewness, ex:kurtosis ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:l4-moment-lab-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "R lab: moments, transformations and distribution checks"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
